    level: "B1",
    text: "Life Among the Leaves — Exploring the Jungle\n\nThe jungle is one of the most biodiverse places on Earth. From the forest floor to the top of the canopy, every layer is full of life.\n\nThe canopy — the thick roof of leaves at the top — blocks most of the sunlight. Up here, toucans call to each other with their enormous colourful beaks, and parrots fly between the branches. Monkeys swing from vine to vine, and a sloth hangs upside down from a branch, moving so slowly that moss grows on its fur. An orangutan — found only in the jungles of Southeast Asia — builds a new nest in the trees every night.\n\nBelow the canopy is the understory — a darker, cooler layer where less light reaches. A chameleon sits on a branch, changing colour to match the leaves around it. A tree frog — bright green or red — clings to a leaf with sticky toes. Butterflies with wings the size of a hand float through the warm air. A dragonfly hovers above a stream. Spiders build webs between fern fronds.\n\nOn the forest floor, the ground is covered with fallen leaves, roots, and mud. An anaconda — one of the largest snakes in the world — coils around the trunk of a palm tree near the river. A tapir — a shy animal with a short trunk — walks through the clearing looking for fruit. Columns of ants march across the ground, carrying pieces of leaf many times their own size. Beetles, centipedes, scorpions, and termites live in the fallen wood. At night, fireflies glow in the darkness, and grasshoppers chirp from every direction.\n\nThe plants are as remarkable as the animals. Bamboo grows up to a metre per day. Orchids bloom on the sides of tree trunks. Ferns spread their broad leaves to catch whatever sunlight filters through. Lianas — thick, woody vines — climb from the forest floor to the canopy. The roots of some trees grow above the ground like buttresses, supporting trunks that rise forty metres high.\n\nWater is everywhere. A waterfall crashes into a lagoon. A river winds through a ravine between steep cliffs. Streams feed into swamps where the water is still and warm. A cave behind the waterfall is home to bats and insects.\n\nA jaguar — the jungle's top predator — rests on a rock near the river. Its spotted coat blends perfectly with the dappled light on the forest floor. An iguana suns itself on a cliff edge above. Caterpillars eat leaves, growing fatter each day before transforming into butterflies. Even mosquitoes have a role — as food for frogs, dragonflies, and bats.\n\nThe jungle is noisy, humid, and crowded. It is also fragile. When trees are cut down, the animals that depend on them lose their homes. Protecting the jungle means protecting millions of species — including many that scientists have not yet discovered.",
